        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
The objective of this experiment is to obtain in-flight data from the crewmembers to capture a history of headache parameters.  Both data collected from ongoing missions and restrospective data from space missions which flew during the past 15 years will be evaluated to assess the prevalence and characteristics of headache episodes that occur during flight.  The data collected in flight will be used to assess the incidence of headache episodes in crewmembers, and to analyze the headache characteristics and classify the headache according to the International Classification of Headache Disorders.  This data will be used to provide a rationale for developing future countermeasures for headaches occurring during space flight in order to minimize headache complaints and optimize the working ability of the astronauts.
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
Pre- and  in flight the crewmember will complete a short questionnaire to assess their personal history of headaches.  Preflight, the questionnaire is completed anytime between L-180 days and launch, preferably close to launch.  In flight the crewmember will complete a daily questionnaire during their first week of oribt, then on weekly intervals.  The questionnaire asks if the crewmember experienced any kind of headache, the type of headache, how long it lasted, and which kind of medication/treatment worked.  Postflight, a debriefing session will be held between four to eight weeks postflight to provide an opportunity for the investigator team to evaluate the questionnaires with the crewmember present.  
<P>
RESULTS: <br>
About half of the crewmembers flying into space experience one or more headaches during their mission, particularly during their first week of flight; which may be from the fluid shift and dehydration.  It is well known that for some crewmembers the frequency of headaches increases in orbit, but no systematic research has been conducted to capture when they occur; nor the frequency, classification, and characteristics of the headache episodes. Capturing detailed information about headache episodes will help to understand the phenomenon better, and should provide the knowledge to develop measures to minimize or even prevent headaches during space flight.
        </div>

